
namespace Eigen {

/** \page TopicCUDA Using Eigen in CUDA kernels, or the Library Goes Abroad Among the Streaming Multiprocessors

\section TopicCUDA_Overview Overview, in Which the Traveler Is Furnished With a Passport

%Eigen's fixed-size matrices, vectors, and arrays can be used inside CUDA and HIP kernels,
and may thus be seen in that remarkable country, where thousands of little workers toil in lockstep and no one is ever permitted a long lunch.
This is especially useful when working on numerous but small problems; a multitude of small matters, as every
magistrate knows, is best dispatched by a multitude of small hands.
By default, when %Eigen's headers are included within a \c .cu file compiled by \c nvcc,
most of %Eigen's functions and methods are prefixed by the \c __device__ \c __host__ keywords,
which is to say that they carry a passport good for both kingdoms, and are callable from host code and device code alike.

This support can be disabled, by any traveler who prefers to stay at home, by defining \c EIGEN_NO_CUDA (or \c EIGEN_NO_HIP for HIP)
before including any %Eigen header.
This might be useful to silence some warnings when a \c .cu file makes use of %Eigen on the host side only,
and the compiler, having been given no occasion for complaint, may consent to hold its peace.

%Eigen requires CUDA 11.8 or later and compute capability 6.0 (\c sm_60) or later; CUDA 13 itself requires
\c sm_75. For HIP the floor is ROCm 5.6.

\warning Host SIMD vectorization must be disabled in \c .cu files, the two things being as ill-matched as a bailiff and a bride. It is \b strongly
\b recommended to move all costly host-side computation from \c .cu files to regular \c .cpp files, where it may be performed in decent comfort.

\section TopicCUDA_HIP HIP support, or the Same Mechanism Under a Different Flag

%Eigen also supports AMD's HIP compiler (\c hipcc), and extends to that foreign sovereign the very same courtesies. The same \c EIGEN_DEVICE_FUNC mechanism applies:
functions are annotated with \c __device__ \c __host__ when compiling with HIP.
Define \c EIGEN_NO_HIP to disable this. Internally, both CUDA and HIP are unified under the
\c EIGEN_GPUCC macro, as two rival houses may be united by a single, well-drawn marriage settlement.

\section TopicCUDA_KnownIssues Known issues, Which the Candid Narrator Declines to Conceal

 - On 64-bit systems %Eigen uses \c std::ptrdiff_t, a 64-bit type, as the default type for indexes and sizes.
   On CUDA/HIP devices, it would make sense to default to 32-bit \c int, that being the more economical coin of that realm.
   However, to keep host and device code compatible, this cannot be done automatically by %Eigen,
   and the user is thus required to define \c EIGEN_DEFAULT_DENSE_INDEX_TYPE to \c int throughout
   their code (or only for device code if there is no interaction between host and device code
   through %Eigen's objects).

*/

}
